===Etymology 2===
From {{inh|en|enm|envirounen}}, {{m|enm|enviroun|t=to surround in a circle or ring, or on the perimeter; to beset, besiege; to cover, enclose, envelop; to provide a setting or surrounding to; to move in a circle; to move around the perimeter; to go, move, or wander about (a place); to fill or pervade (a place); to run all the way through}}{{nb...|enueroun, enuerown, enueyron, enuiron, enuyron, enuyronne, enuyroun, enuyrounne, enuyrowne, enverom, enveron, enveroun, enverounen, enverroun, environ, environne, enviroune, envirowne, envyron, envyrone, envyroun, envyroune, envyrown|otherforms=1}},<ref>{{R:MED Online|entry=envīrǒunen|pos=v|id=MED14191}}</ref> from {{der|en|xno|envirouner}}{{nb...|enviruner, envyronner|otherforms=1}}, {{der|en|frm|environner}}, and {{der|en|fro|environner|t=to arrange in a circle; to circumnavigate, travel around; to traverse, wander around; to encircle, encompass, surround}}{{nb...|enviruner|otherforms=1}} (modern {{cog|fr|environner}}), from {{m|fro|environ|pos=adverb}} (see [[#Etymology 1|etymology 1]])<ref>{{R:OED Online|pos=v|id=63080|date=September 2022|nodot=1}}; {{R:Lexico|pos=v}}</ref> + {{m|fro|-er|pos={{glossary|suffix}} forming {{glossary|verb}}s}}.

